#{[17592186497297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497297 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497297 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497297 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497297 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b6295df7-9006-5cdd-b997-240187450a04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497319 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497319 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497319 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497319 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb7943f0-b18a-5a1c-9e1c-77a8a3b53234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186497325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497324 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "d603d340-e01d-5e3d-8700-e4c91221a0f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186497448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186497456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186497457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186497476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186497484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1085 "To make $x$ cakes, a pastry shop uses the following amounts of ingredients:\n\n- $\\frac{5x+3}{2}$ packs of chocolate\n\n- $\\frac{4x+1}{6}$ packs of butter\n\n- $\\frac{13x+5}{20}$ packs of flour" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "641ef0d3-29e1-5b1f-8a4e-97ad97f061b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497325 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186497448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1071 "Which slice ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) of the cake slices from part 1 corresponds to a right angle?\n\nSlice$\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497333 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 1, we found that $z={7}^{∘}.$ \n\nWe substitute it into the expression for angles and obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =23({7}^{∘})+{4}^{∘}={165}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & =12({7}^{∘})+{6}^{∘}={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & =15({7}^{∘})={105}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, slice $B$ corresponds to a right angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1071 "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\nThe pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497364 #edb/ref 1074 "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $(\\frac{5x+3}{2})$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1071 "How many cakes does the pastry shop produce if they use equal numbers of packs of flour and butter?\n\nThe pastry shop produces$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497390 #edb/ref 1074 "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses the following amounts:\n\n* $\\frac{4x+1}{6}$ packs of butter\n\n* $\\frac{13x+5}{20}$ packs of flour\n\nIf the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+1}{6}=\\frac{13x+5}{20}.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x+1}{6} & =\\frac{13x+5}{20} \\\\ (4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5) \\\\ 80x+20 & =78x+30 \\\\ 80x+20-78x & =78x+30-78x \\\\ 2x+20 & =30 \\\\ 2x+20-20 & =30-20 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $x=5$ cakes are produced when the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/04/04676bef8cc37d05e404dc03211968ebaf39f7adbfc54a4a2cb4d5f8cdc3f1a9.png)\n\nA circular cake is cut into three slices, as shown above. Given that $m∠A=23z+{4}^{∘},$ $m∠B=12z+{6}^{∘},$ and $m∠C=15z,$ then$\\,z=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497402 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the entire cake corresponds to the full angle of ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nBy the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\nm∠A+m∠B+m∠C={360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting the expressions for $m∠A,$ $m∠B,$ and $m∠C,$ and solving for $z,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (23z+{4}^{∘})+(12z+{6}^{∘})+(15z) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z+{10}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z & ={350}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={7}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1071 "The pastry shop's total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) from selling cakes can be modeled by the equations $y=1\\,600+5n$ and $y=64+17n,$ respectively, where $n$ is the number of cakes sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of cakes needed to break even and the revenue made.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,600+5n \\\\ y=64+17n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pastry shop breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes are produced. \n\nThe revenue made when selling this number of cakes is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497411 #edb/ref 1074 "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $1\\,600+5n$ in the second equation and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n-64 & =64+17n-64 \\\\ 1\\,536+5n & =17n \\\\ 1\\,536+5n-5n & =17n-5n \\\\ 1\\,536 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{1\\,536}{12} & =\\frac{12n}{12} \\\\ 128 & =n \\\\ n & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $n=128$ into $y=64+17n$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=64+17(128)=2\\,240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $n=128$ and $y=2\\,240.$\nTherefore, the pastry shop breaks even when $128$ units are produced, and the revenue made when selling this number of units is $$2\\,240.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497441 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61cb8af8-6aaa-5731-948a-249a87d1c6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497441 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497448 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497448 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a61747e-c38c-584e-8963-46aaeb9e15fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497448 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497456 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c67644a-ba6b-5056-a110-d30abc262ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497456 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497457 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497457 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "982a0197-ff18-5665-b059-933e7b15ad60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497457 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497476 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497476 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9a6b498-523d-5ae1-a73c-a7d6564a5ea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497476 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497484 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497484 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef5b46fd-2c6e-5377-86f7-547954d00327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497484 #edb/r